eadline":"37.g4! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The next best move, 37.Qf4+, would drop White's winning chances from 75% to 53% (+0.34).","37.g4 stops the mate threat …Qxg3+.","Black was threatening …Qxg3+, with mate in 2. g4 takes care of it.","The engine's plan continues with Kh3 and Nxh4: this move is the first step.","Moves a pawn from in front of the king, loosening its cover.","Black answers 37…Qf8."]},{"ply":97,"name":"49.Qf5","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"49.Qf5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Shores up the f3 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1).","The next best move, 49.Ne5, would drop White's winning chances from 93% to 79% (+3.67).","49.Qf5 threatens Qxf6, winning the queen, and stops …Rxf3+.","It threatens Qxf6, winning the queen, so Black has to spend the next move on it.","Black was threatening …Rxf3+, winning a pawn. Qf5 takes care of it.","Black answers 49…Qxd4, winning a pawn."]}],"opening":{"name":"Nimzo-Larsen Attack: Modern Variation","eco":"A01","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 9.Ne2: 17 moves in all, 9 by White and 8 by Black.","Theory runs out after 9.Ne2: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}